+{"schemaVersion":"1","kind":"finding","id":"fnd_01M2G2AGX1GZ9THG6HJ1VPP1EP","supersedes":"fnd_01M2G2AGX1GZ9THG6HJ1VPP1EP","recordedAt":"2026-09-14T21:12:30Z","companyId":"example-co","title":"Open EJSON filter/pipeline/document arguments and unbounded database/collection names reach the MongoDB driver directly","description":"zEJSON() (src/tools/args.ts:86-90) is z.object({}).loose().transform(toEJSON) with no keys, maxItems, maxLength or pattern, and is reused unmodified as the filter argument on find (find.ts) and delete-many (deleteMany.ts), the pipeline argument on aggregate (mongodbSchemas.ts/aggregate.ts), the documents argument on insert-many (insertMany.ts: z.array(zEJSON())), and the exportTarget.arguments on export (export.ts). CollOperationArgs.database/.collection (mongodbTool.ts:11-18) are plain z.string() with no bound. All flow straight into the mongosh service-provider driver (provider.find/aggregate/insertMany/deleteMany) with no further schema-level validation; the only backstops are the operator-configured maxBytesPerQuery/maxDocumentsPerQuery caps and the confirmationRequiredTools elicitation list (userConfig.ts:79-94), which excludes insert-many and update-many. Severity is the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 PR.IP.S2. SLA topic: patch-sla. Refutation: evidence lens not refuted (The evidence holds up: the SARIF export has an SCH-API-02 fail result at src/tools/args.ts:86-90 with the same message, controls and fingerprint as the candidate. The checkout shows the same code: zEJSON() = z.object({}).loose().transform(toEJSON), used by find, delete-many, update-many, insert-many (z.array), aggregate (AnyAggregateStage) and export. database and collection are plain z.string(). The values reach provider.find/aggregate/insertMany/deleteMany. confirmationRequiredTools defaults leave out insert-many and update-many. Nothing in the repo cancels this: readOnly and indexCheck are off by default, and deploy/aws/Dockerfile only disables atlas-local. prod.json runs on AWS EKS with partner exposure, so the gap is reachable. medium equals the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 PR.IP.S2 and of dpdp-rules-2025 6(1)(g). A small omission, not grounds to refute: the description skips runtime guards that operators can turn on (readOnly, disabledTools, and aggregate's check that blocks $out/$merge when writes are disabled). These are runtime checks, not schema bounds. PR.IP.S2 is an SDLC control and a loose fit for input validation, but that belongs to the regulatory-mapping lens. (unverifiable: the export sha256 ac53f2a6... cannot be recomputed here.)); regulatory-mapping lens not refuted (The mapping holds. Both Indian instruments apply to example-co: sebi-cscrf-2024 and dpdp-rules-2025 are in frameworksInScope, the company is a stock broker and DP registered as a mid-size RE, and dpdp-rules-2025 lists both entity types. The SEBI control PR.IP.S2 is cited first; it exists, has no reCategory restriction, and its prose asks for an SSDLC with data-integrity requirements and OWASP ASVS guidance, which covers unbounded schemas reaching the MongoDB driver. Severity medium is correct: the SCH-API-02 rule has no security-severity score, so the catalog defaultSeverity of PR.IP.S2 (medium) applies. The controlIds match maxwell/controlIds on the SARIF result at src/tools/args.ts:86-90, and ledger control records exist for both. Minor notes, none reason to refute: (1) PR.IP.S6 (secure coding practices, also medium, also in the ledger) would be a slightly tighter SEBI clause; (2) the 6(1)(g) guidance says findings there usually accompany a pattern of gaps rather than one technical defect, and DPDP Rules take effect 2027-05-13, after now, so it can only be a secondary readiness ref, which is where it sits. Unverifiable: owasp-asvs-5.0 2.2.1 has no local catalog, and SLA fields were not checked, as instructed.).","severity":"medium","confidence":"possible","controlIds":["sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.IP.S2","dpdp-rules-2025:6(1)(g)"],